1) Deadlock Debugging
The deadlock occurs because:
P1 locks R1 and waits for R2.
P2 locks R2 and waits for R1.
Each process holds one resource while waiting for the other, creating a circular wait.
Since neither process releases its first lock, both remain blocked forever.
This satisfies the four deadlock conditions:
Mutual Exclusion
Hold and Wait
No Preemption
Circular Wait
Optimization to Eliminate Deadlock
Use a consistent resource ordering:
Both processes should always lock R1 first and then R2.
This removes the circular wait condition without changing the program's logic.
Alternatively, use try-lock with timeout to release held resources and retry if the second lock is unavailable
2) CPU Utilization Problem
Cause of Low CPU Utilization
Although processes are ready, they frequently transition:
Ready → Waiting → Ready → Waiting
This indicates that processes are I/O-bound.
  They execute briefly and immediately request I/O.
  The CPU becomes idle while waiting for I/O completion.
  Frequent context switching also wastes CPU cycles.
System-Level Optimization
Increase multiprogramming level
Run more processes simultaneously so another process uses the CPU while one waits for I/O.
Improve I/O scheduling
Use asynchronous I/O, buffering, or caching to reduce waiting time.
Use a scheduler optimized for I/O-bound processes
Quickly schedule another ready process whenever one blocks.
These optimizations increase CPU utilization and improve overall system throughput.
3) Memory Bottleneck Debugging
Memory Issue
Memory usage:
Code = 5 MB
Heap = 150 MB
Stack = 1 MB × 25 threads = 25 MB
Total memory ≈ 180 MB
Even though physical RAM is 2 GB, the program experiences frequent page faults, indicating poor memory access patterns.
Possible reasons:
Large heap causes random memory accesses.
Many threads compete for memory, reducing cache efficiency.
Frequent page swapping increases page faults and slows execution.
Optimizations
Reduce memory usage
Optimize heap allocations.
Reuse memory using object pools.
Reduce unnecessary data structures.
Reduce the number of threads
Use a thread pool instead of creating many threads.
Lower memory consumption and improve cache locality.
These optimizations reduce page faults and improve execution speed.
4)Starvation Debugging
Root Cause
Process P5 is in Q3 (Batch Queue), which has the lowest priority.
The scheduler always executes:
Q1 (Interactive) first
Then Q2 (System)
Finally Q3 (Batch)
If Q1 and Q2 continuously receive new processes, Q3 never gets CPU time, causing starvation.
Optimization for Fairness
Priority Aging
Gradually increase the priority of waiting processes.
Eventually, P5 moves to a higher-priority queue and gets CPU time.
Allocate CPU time to lower queues
Reserve a fixed percentage of CPU time for Q3.
Prevents indefinite postponement.
These methods eliminate starvation while maintaining priority scheduling.
5)Context-Switch Overhead Debugging
Reason for High Context Switching
The server performs 20,000 context switches per second because:
Time quantum is only 1 ms.
Average CPU burst is 2 ms, so most threads are preempted before completing.
Very frequent I/O operations cause threads to block often.
Every preemption and I/O event triggers another context switch, increasing overhead.
Optimizations
Increase the Time Quantum
Increase the quantum from 1 ms to 4–5 ms.
Threads complete more work before being preempted, reducing context switches.
Reduce I/O Waiting
Use asynchronous or buffered I/O.
Batch I/O requests where possible.
Fewer blocking operations result in fewer context switches.
These optimizations reduce scheduling overhead, improve CPU efficiency, and increase system performance.
